===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|party}}, {{m|enm|partye}}, {{m|enm|partie}}, from {{der|en|xno|partie}}, from {{der|en|fro|partie||side, part; portion, share; separation, division|lit=that which is divided}}, noun use of feminine of past participle of {{der|en|fro|partir|t=to divide, separate}}, from {{der|en|la|partire||to share, part, distribute, divide}}, from {{m|la|pars|t=a part, piece, a share}}; see also [[part]]. {{etydate|c|1300}}. {{doublet|en|partita}}.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> {{surf|+suf|en|part|y|pos2=noun suffix}}.
* {{senseno|en|Communist Party|t=the [[Communist Party]]|cap=1}} is a {{semantic loan|en|ru|партия|nocap=1}}, short for {{m|ru|Коммунистическая партия}}. {{rfv-etym|en}}

====Noun====
[[File:Birthday party.jpg|thumb|A [[birthday]] '''party''' ''({{senseno|en|fun|etym=false}}'') for a child.]]

{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} A [[person]] or [[group]] of people constituting one side in a legal proceeding, such as in a [[legal action]] or a [[contract]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1300s}}
#: {{ux|en|The contract requires that the '''party''' of the first part pay the fee.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1612|author=w:Sir John Davies|title=Discoverie of the True Causes why Ireland was never entirely subdued
|passage=if the Jury had found that the '''party''' slain had been of English race and nation, it had been adjudged felony}}
# {{lb|en|dated|slang|vulgar|or|humorous}} A [[person]]; an [[individual]]. {{defdate|from after 1475}}
#: {{ux|en|He is a queer '''party'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|page=135|passage='These young '''parties''' have a way of looking at one, sir,' he would say apologetically, 'which I don't call respectable.'}}
## With '''[[to]]''': someone who takes part in an [[action]] or [[affair]]; an [[accessory]].
##: {{ux|en|I can't possibly be (a) [[party to|'''party''' to]] that kind of reckless behaviour.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare|_|in general sense}} A [[group]] of people forming one side in a given [[dispute]], [[contest]], etc., or united in maintaining a [[cause]], [[policy]], or [[opinion]] in opposition to others; a [[faction]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1320s}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Tarzan of the Apes|6|passage=A mile back in the forest the tribe had heard the fierce challenge of the gorilla, and, as was his custom when any danger threatened, Kerchak called his people together, partly for mutual protection against a common enemy, since this gorilla might be but one of a '''party''' of several, and also to see that all members of the tribe were accounted for.}}
## {{senseid|en|political group}}{{lb|en|politics}} A [[political]] [[group]] considered as a formal whole, usually organized on a [[national]] basis, which contests [[elections]] and aims to form or take part in a [[government]]; a [[political party]]. {{defdate|from 1680s}}
##: {{ux|en|The green '''party''' took 12% of the vote.}}
##: {{ux|en|His '''party''' was campaigning successfuly.}}
##* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=A Lady in Company|page=21|text="A fine man, that Dunwody, yonder," commented the young captain, as they parted, and as he turned to his prisoner. "We'll see him on in Washington some day.{{...}}A strong man&mdash;a strong one; and a heedless." ¶ "Of what '''party''' is he?" she inquired, as though casually.}}
##* {{RQ:Orwell Nineteen Eighty-Four|chapter=7|page=76|passage=The '''Party''' told you to reject the evidence of your eyes and ears. It was their final, most essential command.}}
##* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1996
|author=w:John Gittings
|chapter=Peasants in Revolt
|title=Real China: From Cannibalism to Karaoke
|url=https://archive.org/details/realchinafromcan0000gitt/
|publisher=w:Pocket Books
|year_published=1997
|ISBN=0-671-51651-5
|OCLC=38996810
|page=46
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/realchinafromcan0000gitt/page/46/
|text=In the freer political atmosphere before the 1989 repression, peasant protests against the '''Party''' in Henan were sometimes openly discussed. A transcript of a meeting between peasants and cadres in Jili district near Luoyang published in the ''Peasants’ Daily'' recorded some vivid complaints.}}
##* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Fredreka Schouten and Arit John|title=The DNC’s cash crunch deepens as new filings show Republicans with a huge advantage|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/06/21/politics/dnc-financial-troubles-fec-filings|date=2025-06-21|passage=The RNC entered June with about $72.4 million in cash reserves, nearly five times the $15 million stockpiled by the DNC, as the two '''parties''' gear up for gubernatorial races this fall and next year’s costly midterm elections for Congress. {{...}} The '''party'''’s cash crunch is serious enough that '''party''' officials have weighed seeking a line of credit to help it stay afloat, a development first reported by The New York Times.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250816035510/https://www.cnn.com/2025/06/21/politics/dnc-financial-troubles-fec-filings|archivedate=16 August 2025}}
